WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.056
You learn that a bond in your portfolio is issued by

00:03.156 --> 00:07.001
an energy company. Should that change your estimate of the chance

00:07.101 --> 00:11.372
that it gets downgraded this year? Almost certainly yes. New information

00:11.472 --> 00:14.528
changes probabilities. The question is: by how much?

00:16.616 --> 00:20.226
The answer is one simple picture: conditioning means zooming in.

00:21.316 --> 00:24.638
Draw every bond in the portfolio as a square of area

00:24.738 --> 00:28.586
one. The energy bonds are this column, thirty percent of the

00:28.686 --> 00:32.862
area. The bonds that will be downgraded are shaded yellow. Notice

00:32.962 --> 00:36.942
there is more yellow inside the energy column than outside it.

00:37.033 --> 00:40.006
Before we know anything, the chance of a downgrade

00:40.106 --> 00:43.202
is the total yellow area: about twenty-nine percent.

00:43.300 --> 00:47.025
Now we learn the bond is an energy bond. Everything outside the

00:47.125 --> 00:50.850
column is no longer possible. So throw it away, and stretch the

00:50.950 --> 00:54.615
column until it is our whole world again, with total area one.

00:54.700 --> 00:58.203
In this new world, what share is yellow? Half. So

00:58.303 --> 01:01.806
given that the bond is an energy bond, the chance

01:01.906 --> 01:05.997
of a downgrade is fifty percent. That's the definition of

01:06.097 --> 01:11.071
conditional probability: the probability of A and B together, divided

01:11.171 --> 01:15.262
by the probability of B. Dividing is just the stretching.

01:16.350 --> 01:19.456
A common mistake is to flip the condition around. The

01:19.556 --> 01:22.541
chance that an energy bond is downgraded is not the

01:22.641 --> 01:25.445
chance that a downgraded bond is an energy bond.

01:25.533 --> 01:28.694
Both use the same overlap, the yellow part of the

01:28.794 --> 01:32.221
column. But one divides by the blue column, the other

01:32.321 --> 01:36.946
by all the yellow. Different denominators, different answers. Here they

01:37.046 --> 01:40.207
happen to be close; in many real problems, like a

01:40.307 --> 01:44.267
rare disease and a common symptom, they are wildly different.

01:46.366 --> 01:49.442
Now imagine a different portfolio, where the yellow band has

01:49.542 --> 01:52.353
the same height inside the energy column as outside it.

01:52.450 --> 01:56.009
Then zooming in changes nothing: the share of yellow in the

01:56.109 --> 01:59.916
column equals the share of yellow overall. Learning B tells you

02:00.016 --> 02:03.452
nothing about A. That is exactly what independence means.

02:04.550 --> 02:08.619
One last trap. Events that can't happen together, that don't

02:08.719 --> 02:12.650
overlap at all, are not independent. They are as dependent

02:12.750 --> 02:15.429
as it gets: if B happens, A is ruled out

02:15.529 --> 02:18.974
completely. Zooming into B leaves no yellow at all.

02:20.066 --> 02:23.438
So remember the picture. Conditioning on B means throwing

02:23.538 --> 02:26.788
away everything outside B, and rescaling what's left to

02:26.888 --> 02:30.321
have total probability one. And two events are independent

02:30.421 --> 02:33.367
exactly when that zoom doesn't change the picture.
